WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.146
Let's walk across an atoll rim, from the open ocean to the lagoon.

00:00:05.246 --> 00:00:09.054
On the ocean side, the reef front drops steeply into deep water.

00:00:09.054 --> 00:00:13.218
Waves break on the reef crest, often cemented by pink coralline algae.

00:00:13.218 --> 00:00:18.334
Behind it lies the reef flat, a shallow platform a few hundred metres wide where waves

00:00:18.334 --> 00:00:19.167
move sediment.

00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:22.795
Then comes a storm ridge of coarse rubble, the island itself,

00:00:22.795 --> 00:00:24.937
and finally the calmer lagoon beach.

00:00:26.037 --> 00:00:28.165
Cyclones build as well as destroy.

00:00:28.165 --> 00:00:33.110
In 1972 Cyclone Bebe threw up a long bank of rubble on Funafuti's eastern reef.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:37.179
Over the following decades waves reworked it onto the shorelines,

00:00:37.179 --> 00:00:38.994
and the eastern islands grew.

00:00:40.094 --> 00:00:44.766
Fresh water on an atoll floats on seawater inside the porous rock, as a thin lens.

00:00:44.766 --> 00:00:49.553
By the Ghyben–Herzberg rule, for every metre the water table stands above sea level,

00:00:49.553 --> 00:00:52.459
the fresh lens reaches about forty metres below it.

00:00:52.459 --> 00:00:56.162
On a narrow islet the lens is thin and easily spoiled by drought,

00:00:56.162 --> 00:00:58.670
over-pumping or waves washing over the land.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:04.150
On Fogafale the lens is exposed in pits dug for wartime airfield fill,

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:07.404
and in pits used to grow pulaka, a giant swamp taro.

00:01:07.404 --> 00:01:10.033
When salt water gets in, the gardens fail.

00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:13.725
That is one of the first visible effects of sea-level rise.
